   "glossary": [
    {
     "term": "Investment Company Act of 1940",
     "def": "Federal law that defines, classifies, and regulates investment companies; requires SEC registration and prospectus delivery."
    },
    {
     "term": "Open-End Fund (Mutual Fund)",
     "def": "A management company with unlimited capitalization that continuously issues and redeems its own shares at NAV plus any sales charge."
    },
    {
     "term": "Closed-End Fund",
     "def": "A management company with a fixed number of shares that trade on an exchange or OTC at a premium or discount to NAV based on supply and demand."
    },
    {
     "term": "Unit Investment Trust (UIT)",
     "def": "An investment company with a fixed, unmanaged portfolio and no board or investment adviser; sells redeemable units, often with a set termination date."
    },
    {
     "term": "Net Asset Value (NAV)",
     "def": "The per-share value of a fund: (total assets − total liabilities) ÷ shares outstanding, computed once per business day at the close."
    },
    {
     "term": "Public Offering Price (POP)",
     "def": "The price an investor pays for mutual fund shares: NAV plus the applicable sales charge; equals NAV for a no-load fund."
    },
    {
     "term": "Forward Pricing",
     "def": "The rule that mutual fund buy and redemption orders are executed at the next NAV calculated after the order is received."
    },
    {
     "term": "Sales Charge (Load)",
     "def": "A fee added to NAV to compensate selling firms; FINRA caps it at 8.5% of the POP on a mutual fund."
    },
    {
     "term": "Class A Shares",
     "def": "Shares with a front-end sales charge paid at purchase, the lowest 12b-1 fees, and eligibility for breakpoints; best for large, long-term investors."
    },
    {
     "term": "Class B Shares",
     "def": "Shares with a contingent deferred sales charge (CDSC) that declines over time; higher 12b-1 fees; typically convert to Class A after the CDSC period."
    },
    {
     "term": "Class C Shares",
     "def": "Level-load shares with little or no front/back load but the highest ongoing 12b-1 fee; best for shorter holding periods."
    },
    {
     "term": "Contingent Deferred Sales Charge (CDSC)",
     "def": "A back-end sales charge (typical of Class B shares) assessed on redemption that decreases each year and eventually reaches zero."
    },
    {
     "term": "Breakpoint",
     "def": "A reduced sales-charge percentage on Class A shares granted when an investor's purchase reaches specified dollar thresholds."
    },
    {
     "term": "Letter of Intent (LOI)",
     "def": "A non-binding statement to invest enough to reach a breakpoint within 13 months (may be backdated up to 90 days); shares are escrowed as security."
    },
    {
     "term": "Rights of Accumulation (ROA)",
     "def": "A benefit allowing an investor to count the value of existing holdings toward a breakpoint on new purchases, with no time limit."
    },
    {
     "term": "Breakpoint Sale",
     "def": "A prohibited practice of selling shares just below a breakpoint to earn a higher commission instead of helping the client qualify for the discount."
    },
    {
     "term": "12b-1 Fee",
     "def": "An annual fee charged against fund assets to cover distribution/marketing costs; max 0.75% distribution plus 0.25% service; reviewed annually by the board."
    },
    {
     "term": "Expense Ratio",
     "def": "A fund's annual operating expenses (management fee + 12b-1 + administrative costs) divided by average net assets; excludes sales charges."
    },
    {
     "term": "7-Day Rule",
     "def": "An open-end fund must pay redemption proceeds within seven calendar days of receiving a request in proper form."
    }
   ],
   "cheatSheet": [
    "The Investment Company Act of 1940 defines three types: management companies (open-end + closed-end), UITs, and face-amount certificate companies.",
    "Open-end = unlimited shares, redeemable, priced at NAV + sales charge (forward priced); closed-end = fixed shares, exchange-traded, premium/discount to NAV.",
    "A UIT has a fixed, unmanaged portfolio with no investment adviser and no board of directors.",
    "NAV = (assets − liabilities) ÷ shares outstanding; computed once daily at the close.",
    "POP = NAV ÷ (1 − sales charge %); the sales charge percentage is taken on the POP, not the NAV.",
    "Forward pricing: orders fill at the NEXT computed NAV, never the last-quoted price.",
    "Maximum mutual fund sales charge = 8.5% of POP (only if the fund offers breakpoints, ROA, and reinvestment at NAV).",
    "Class A = front-end load + breakpoints (large/long-term); Class B = CDSC that declines; Class C = level load, highest 12b-1 (short-term).",
    "A fund may call itself 'no-load' only if it has no front/back load and 12b-1 fees ≤ 0.25%.",
    "Letter of Intent = 13 months, backdatable 90 days; Rights of Accumulation = no time limit, uses existing holdings.",
    "A breakpoint sale (selling just under a breakpoint to boost commission) is a prohibited violation.",
    "A 12b-1 fee is an ANNUAL distribution/marketing fee (max 0.75% + 0.25% service), not an up-front load; the board reviews it at least annually.",
    "Custodian safeguards assets; transfer agent issues/redeems shares and keeps records; adviser runs the portfolio (largest fee).",
    "7-day rule: redemption proceeds must be paid within 7 calendar days at the next-computed NAV.",
    "At least 40% of a fund's board of directors must be non-interested (independent)."
   ],
