    {
     "heading": "Options Accounts, Death of an Account Holder & ACATS",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<p>An <strong>options account</strong> carries added risk and leverage, so it requires extra disclosure and specialized approval before the first trade.</p>"
      },
      {
       "type": "steps",
       "title": "Opening an Options Account",
       "steps": [
        {
         "label": "Gather suitability information",
         "desc": "The rep collects the customer's financial situation, experience, and objectives and assigns an approval level."
        },
        {
         "label": "Deliver the ODD",
         "desc": "The Options Disclosure Document must be delivered at or before the time the account is approved for options."
        },
        {
         "label": "Principal approval",
         "desc": "A Registered Options Principal (ROP) must approve the account in writing before the first options trade."
        },
        {
         "label": "Trading may begin",
         "desc": "Once the ODD is delivered and the account is approved, the customer may enter options orders."
        },
        {
         "label": "Return the signed agreement",
         "desc": "The signed options agreement must be returned within 15 days of account approval."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "type": "callout",
       "style": "exam",
       "title": "On the exam...",
       "text": "Sequence matters: the ODD comes at or before approval, but the signed options agreement is due back WITHIN 15 DAYS of approval. So a customer may trade options before signing the agreement — but never before receiving the ODD."
      },
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<p>When an <strong>account holder dies</strong>, the firm follows a strict freeze-and-document procedure.</p>"
      },
      {
       "type": "key",
       "title": "Steps at Death of an Account Holder",
       "items": [
        "CANCEL all open orders immediately",
        "Mark the account DECEASED and freeze activity",
        "Void any power of attorney or trading authorization — they die with the owner",
        "Await the required documents (death certificate, court/estate papers, inheritance tax waivers if applicable) before releasing or retitling assets"
       ]
      },
      {
       "type": "callout",
       "style": "tip",
       "title": "Inherited securities",
       "text": "Securities passing at death generally receive a STEPPED-UP cost basis equal to fair market value on the date of death. Any subsequent sale by the heir is treated as long-term regardless of how long the heir actually held them."
      },
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<p>To move an account between firms, the industry uses <strong>ACATS</strong> — the Automated Customer Account Transfer Service, governed by FINRA <strong>Rule 11870</strong>. The customer signs a Transfer Initiation Form (TIF) at the <em>receiving</em> firm.</p>"
      },
      {
       "type": "steps",
       "title": "How an ACATS Transfer Works",
       "steps": [
        {
         "label": "TIF submitted",
         "desc": "The receiving (new) firm sends the customer's transfer instruction to the carrying (old) firm through ACATS."
        },
        {
         "label": "Validate within 1 business day",
         "desc": "The carrying firm must validate the asset list or take exception with a reason within one business day."
        },
        {
         "label": "Complete within 3 business days",
         "desc": "After validation, the carrying firm must complete the transfer within three business days — roughly a week end to end."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "type": "callout",
       "style": "tip",
       "title": "Non-transferable positions",
       "text": "Some assets — such as a firm's proprietary mutual funds — cannot transfer in kind through ACATS. The customer must instruct the firm to liquidate or retain those positions, while the transferable securities move to the new firm."
      }
     ]
    }
